Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant columns and their potential use
----------------------------------------
"sellers"."seller_id": unique key to aggregate every metric per seller  
"sellers"."seller_city" / "seller_state": optional geographical slicers  

"order_items"."order_id": bridge to both "orders" and "order_reviews"  
"order_items"."seller_id": link to the seller that shipped the item  
"order_items"."product_id": link to the product table for category look‑ups  
"order_items"."price": monetary value of each item – used for total sales and average item price  
"order_items"."order_item_id": every row is one stock keeping unit; COUNT(*) ≈ quantity sold  

"orders"."order_id": main key to merge with items and reviews  
"orders"."order_approved_at": proxy for the moment the seller received the order  
"orders"."order_delivered_carrier_date": proxy for the moment the parcel left the seller’s warehouse  
 •  julianday("order_delivered_carrier_date") - julianday("order_approved_at") → packing time (days)  

"order_reviews"."order_id": join back to Orders/Items  
"order_reviews"."review_score": customer feedback; average it per seller  

"products"."product_id": join key to items  
"products"."product_category_name": Portuguese category code – translate it  

"product_category_name_translation"."product_category_name": join key  
"product_category_name_translation"."product_category_name_english": human‑readable category; highlight the one with highest COUNT(*)  

Conditions we will need
-----------------------
1. GROUP BY "order_items"."seller_id"  
2. HAVING COUNT(*) > 100                          -- keep only large sellers  
3. For category ranking: ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC   -- highest sales volume  

Exploratory / sanity‑check queries
---------------------------------
Below are 10 different SELECT statements (each capped at 20 rows) that let you peek at the essential values before writing the final report.

```sql
-- 1) List a handful of seller IDs
SELECT DISTINCT "seller_id"
FROM "sellers"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2) Inspect distinct seller states for eventual geographic splits
SELECT DISTINCT "seller_state"
FROM "sellers"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3) See the raw product category codes coming from the catalog
SELECT DISTINCT "product_category_name"
FROM "products"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4) Check the available English translations of category names
SELECT DISTINCT "product_category_name_english"
FROM "product_category_name_translation"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5) Count how many units each seller has shipped (quick volume test)
SELECT "seller_id",
       COUNT(*) AS "items_sold"
FROM "order_items"
GROUP BY "seller_id"
HAVING COUNT(*) > 100
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6) What price range do individual items fall into?
SELECT DISTINCT "price"
FROM "order_items"
ORDER BY "price" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7) Distribution of review scores that we’ll later average
SELECT DISTINCT "review_score"
FROM "order_reviews"
ORDER BY "review_score"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8) Sample of packing times (days) calculated from Order timestamps
SELECT "order_id",
       julianday("order_delivered_carrier_date") -
       julianday("order_approved_at") AS "packing_time_days"
FROM "orders"
WHERE "order_delivered_carrier_date" IS NOT NULL
  AND "order_approved_at"           IS NOT NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9) Identify top‑selling product categories in English (global view)
SELECT t."product_category_name_english",
       COUNT(*) AS "items_sold"
FROM "order_items"          AS oi
JOIN "products"             AS p ON p."product_id" = oi."product_id"
JOIN "product_category_name_translation" AS t
     ON t."product_category_name" = p."product_category_name"
GROUP BY t."product_category_name_english"
ORDER BY "items_sold" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--10) Cross‑tab: seller vs. category to see where each big seller excels
SELECT oi."seller_id",
       t."product_category_name_english",
       COUNT(*) AS "items_sold"
FROM "order_items"          AS oi
JOIN "products"             AS p ON p."product_id" = oi."product_id"
JOIN "product_category_name_translation" AS t
     ON t."product_category_name" = p."product_category_name"
GROUP BY oi."seller_id", t."product_category_name_english"
HAVING COUNT(*) > 100
ORDER BY oi."seller_id", "items_sold" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

